45417] [17592186147890 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186145094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147890 #edb/ref 1069 "q-61533" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147890 #edb/ref 1071 "What is $120%$ of $25$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147890 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9bc7c273-0dfe-58b5-98ac-f3b5aaeadbfa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148370 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148370 #edb/ref 1061 54220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148370 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d3e65926-0f7c-5d3a-b5ff-962bec1f0e24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148370 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148371 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148371 #edb/ref 1061 54221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148371 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "092306a2-20ad-5d22-9e48-cb1c1ae8774d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148371 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148372 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148372 #edb/ref 1061 54224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148372 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0674771c-1511-5f16-a17e-54ff8d00c79e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148372 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148373 #edb/ref 1061 54227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148373 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c8e96f68-307a-58c2-8d26-9f11f68fde49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148373 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148789 #edb/ref 1064 "We can find a percentage part of a number using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part} = \\textrm{percentage} \\times \\textrm{whole}\n$$\n\nTo illustrate, let's calculate $16 \\%$ of $300$.\n\nHere, the whole is $300$ and the percentage is $16 \\%$. So, the part is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{part} = 16\\% \\times 300\n$$\n\nBut how do we multiply a percentage by a number? We have two methods of doing this. Let's apply both and check that they give the same answer.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n16\\% = 16 \\div 100 = 0.16\n$$\n\nThen, we apply our formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 16% \\cdot 300 \\\\\n&= 0.16 \\cdot 300 \\\\\n&= 48\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $16\\%$ of $300$ is $48$.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n16\\% = \\dfrac{16}{100}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply our formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\npart &= percentage \\cdot whole \\\\\n&= 16% \\cdot 300 \\\\\n&= \\frac{16}{100} \\cdot 300 \\\\\n&= 16 \\cdot \\frac{300}{100} \\\\\n&= 16 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 48\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $16 \\%$ of $300$ is $48$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148789 #edb/ref 1065 8198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148789 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "41c3207b-f9a6-5e36-9a0b-a6ad0c9e8a18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148789 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
